        "pre_narrative": "The Capital Phantom has struck the Crypts' signage, capitalizing the word after every colon — even when it shouldn't be capitalized. He knows most students don't think about what follows a colon, only what precedes it. A crypt notice now looks oddly formal. Find the correctly capitalized version.",
        "passage": "A crypt notice reads: \"The librarian has left one instruction for visitors[underlined: : Please do not remove scrolls from their protective cases].\"",
        "question_stem": "In the passage above, which version of the underlined portion is correct?",
        "option_a": ": please do not remove scrolls from their protective cases",
        "option_b": ": Please do not remove scrolls from their protective cases",
        "option_c": "; please do not remove scrolls from their protective cases",
        "option_d": ", please do not remove scrolls from their protective cases",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "After a colon, capitalization depends on what follows. If the colon introduces a complete sentence (an independent clause), the first word of that sentence may be capitalized — and in the case of instructions, commands, or formal announcements, capitalization is standard. \"Please do not remove scrolls from their protective cases\" is a complete sentence — an imperative (command) sentence with the implied subject \"you.\" It stands as an independent clause after the colon, so capitalizing \"Please\" is correct. Option B is correct. Option A uses a lowercase \"please\" — while some style guides allow lowercase after a colon even for complete sentences, the ACT generally capitalizes the first word of a complete independent clause following a colon, especially in formal writing. Option C uses a semicolon, but \"please do not remove scrolls\" is not being joined to the first clause as an equal partner — it is being introduced as the content of \"one instruction,\" making a colon more appropriate. Option D uses a comma, which is too weak and informal to introduce a formal instruction.",
        "success_narrative": "The notice is correctly posted. Visitors read the instruction clearly, and no scrolls have been removed since the Capital Phantom was driven from the premises.",
        "failure_narrative": "The Phantom points upward — to the capital letter. When a complete sentence follows a colon, capitalize the first word. \"Please do not remove...\" is a complete command sentence.",
        "xp_reward": 75,
        "rune_reward": 0,
        "concept_tag": "colon_capitalization",
        "difficulty_tag": "standard",
        "act_skill_area": "Punctuation"
