        "correct_answer": "C",
        "explanation": "\"The crates delivered to the Crypts contained\" is NOT a complete sentence. \"Contained\" is a transitive verb that needs its object — the list IS that object. Placing a colon between a verb and its direct object interrupts the sentence structure, which is never allowed. Options A and D both place punctuation (a colon or semicolon) directly after \"contained,\" which breaks the verb away from the object it needs. Option B has no colon — the list simply follows the verb as its object, which is perfectly grammatical, but the question is about correct colon usage. Option C adds \"the following items\" to complete the clause: \"The crates...contained the following items\" — now \"the following items\" is the object of \"contained,\" the clause is complete, and the colon correctly introduces the list. This is the standard ACT-approved way to use a colon when a verb-object construction would otherwise be interrupted.",
        "success_narrative": "The manifest is correctly filled out. The Fragment Forger's broken mirror is among the items — and it stays broken, unlike the grammar of the sentence.",
        "failure_narrative": "The Forger smiles. \"Contained\" needs its object — the list IS the object. Add \"the following items\" to complete the clause before the colon can legally appear.",
