        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "\"The king has made his decision\" is a complete clause that announces a fact without stating what that decision is. It creates a buildup — the reader expects to be told what the decision is. \"The dispute over the northern border will be resolved by the end of the month\" IS that decision. The second clause directly reveals the content of \"his decision.\" This is a classic colon setup: the first clause announces, the second delivers. The colon signals \"here it is\" — the elaboration or content of what was promised. A semicolon (option A) would suggest the two clauses are parallel, equal observations — but they are not equal here. The first clause is a setup, and the second is its payload. The colon in option B captures this relationship precisely. Option C creates a comma splice. Option D uses an em dash, which can work for dramatic effect but is less precise than a colon in formal writing, and the ACT prefers the colon in this setup-delivery pattern.",
        "success_narrative": "The announcement rings through the hall of records. The king's decision is clearly stated, properly introduced by its colon, and the border dispute ends.",
