        "option_c": "no flames in the reading rooms; no removal of artifacts; and absolutely no speaking in the sealed chambers",
        "option_d": "No flames in the reading rooms; No removal of artifacts; and Absolutely no speaking in the sealed chambers",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "After a colon that introduces a list (where the items are NOT full sentences), the first word is typically lowercase. Each item in the list is not a complete independent sentence — they are parallel noun phrases. Option B correctly uses lowercase for \"no flames\" and maintains the standard comma-separated series format. Option A capitalizes \"No\" after the colon — capitalization after a colon is used when the following text is a complete, formal sentence or when items in a list are themselves complete sentences. Since these are noun phrases (not complete sentences), lowercase is correct. Option C uses semicolons to separate the items — semicolons in lists are only required when items have internal commas. These items have no internal commas, so regular commas are correct. Option D both capitalizes incorrectly and uses semicolons incorrectly, making it doubly wrong.",
