        "enemy_type": "Ancient Scribe",
        "pre_narrative": "The Ambiguity Archivist presents a sentence from the Crypts' most contested document — one where scholars have argued for generations about whether a colon or semicolon is correct. He has removed both marks and offers you four options. One is precisely right; two are wrong for different reasons; one might look right but fails on close inspection.",
        "passage": "A contested document reads: \"The expedition succeeded beyond expectations[underlined: : the team recovered not one but seven intact manuscripts from the flooded lower chamber].\"",
        "question_stem": "In the passage above, which version of the underlined portion is correct?",
        "option_a": ": the team recovered not one but seven intact manuscripts from the flooded lower chamber",
        "option_b": "; the team recovered not one but seven intact manuscripts from the flooded lower chamber",
        "option_c": ", the team recovered not one but seven intact manuscripts from the flooded lower chamber",
        "option_d": ". The team recovered not one but seven intact manuscripts from the flooded lower chamber",
        "correct_answer": "A",
        "explanation": "\"The expedition succeeded beyond expectations\" is a complete clause that makes a strong claim — it promises an elaboration. \"The team recovered not one but seven intact manuscripts from the flooded lower chamber\" is the specific fact that explains WHY the expedition succeeded beyond expectations. This is a classic colon relationship: the first clause states a general claim, and the colon introduces the specific evidence or elaboration. The phrase \"beyond expectations\" creates an anticipation that the colon's second clause fulfills. Option A is correct. Option B uses a semicolon — while both clauses are independent, the colon is more precise here because the second clause is not merely a related fact; it is the specific CONTENT of what \"beyond expectations\" means. The colon emphasizes that the second clause delivers what the first clause promised. Option C creates a comma splice. Option D uses a period — grammatically fine, but the period creates a larger break that reduces the sense of immediate fulfillment; the colon signals \"here is exactly why\" with greater immediacy.",
        "success_narrative": "The contested document is resolved at last. The Ambiguity Archivist files the ruling, and seven intact manuscripts are examined by scholars who have waited three generations for this moment.",
        "failure_narrative": "The Archivist gestures at both clauses. \"Beyond expectations\" is a PROMISE — the colon delivers on it immediately. A semicolon would just link two equal facts; a colon says \"here is exactly what that means.\"",
