        "pre_narrative": "The Clause Crypt-Keeper presents the hardest colon test in the Crypts: a sentence where determining whether the clause before the colon is complete requires careful analysis of verb structure. He has struck a scholarly dispatch. One option is clearly wrong; one is subtly wrong; one looks almost right but fails the completeness test; and one is precisely correct.",
        "passage": "A scholarly dispatch reads: \"The three most important factors in scroll preservation [underlined: are: temperature, humidity, and light exposure].\"",
        "question_stem": "In the passage above, which version of the underlined portion is correct?",
        "option_a": "are: temperature, humidity, and light exposure",
        "option_b": "are temperature, humidity, and light exposure",
        "option_c": "are the following: temperature, humidity, and light exposure",
        "option_d": "include the following: temperature, humidity, and light exposure",
        "correct_answer": "C",
        "explanation": "This is the hardest version of the \"complete clause before colon\" rule. \"The three most important factors in scroll preservation are\" — is this a complete clause? \"Are\" is a linking verb, and a linking verb REQUIRES a predicate noun or adjective to complete it. \"Are\" alone, with just a colon, is incomplete. The list IS the predicate, so placing a colon between the linking verb and its predicate nominative (the list) is illegal. Options A and D: Option A places the colon after \"are\" — incomplete. Option D uses \"include the following,\" and \"include\" is a transitive verb that also needs its object — however, \"include\" + \"the following\" creates a slightly awkward construction because \"include\" is slightly different from \"are.\" Option C adds \"the following\" as the predicate of \"are\": \"The three most important factors...are the following\" — now \"the following\" is the predicate nominative that completes \"are,\" making the clause complete. The colon then introduces the list. Option C is precisely correct. Option B omits the colon entirely and lists the factors directly after \"are\" — this is grammatically correct English, but the question tests colon usage, so B cannot be the answer.",
