        "option_c": "; a single unbroken seal bearing the crest of the First Keep",
        "option_d": ", a single unbroken seal bearing the crest of the First Keep",
        "correct_answer": "A",
        "explanation": "Both em dashes and colons can introduce an explanation or identification after a complete clause, but the ACT distinguishes between them in tone and context. An em dash is used for dramatic, sudden, or emphatic emphasis — it signals a pause that heightens anticipation or surprise. A colon is used for more formal, neutral introductions of lists, explanations, or quotations. Here, \"After forty years of searching, the explorers found what they had been looking for\" is a setup that carries enormous emotional weight. The phrase \"what they had been looking for\" specifically creates suspense — the reader wants to know WHAT it is. The em dash creates the dramatic pause that the forty-year search deserves. Option A correctly uses the em dash for this emotional, climactic reveal. Option B uses a colon — grammatically defensible, but the colon is too neutral and formal for the dramatic weight of this moment; on the ACT, when the context signals drama or surprise, the em dash is preferred. Option C uses a semicolon — incorrect, since \"a single unbroken seal bearing the crest of the First Keep\" is not an independent clause. Option D uses a comma — too weak and informal for this construction.",
        "success_narrative": "The announcement echoes through the Crypts. The Dramatic Dash bows in defeat — even he admits that some moments deserve the pause only a dash can deliver.",
