        "correct_answer": "D",
        "explanation": "This is a mastery-level question that tests whether you recognize correctly used colons — not just find errors. Both colons in this passage are correct. First colon: \"The Crypts exist for one purpose\" is a complete clause. \"One purpose\" sets up an expectation that the colon fulfills: \"to preserve the written knowledge of the Keep.\" The colon correctly introduces the explanation of what that purpose is. Second colon: \"Scholars who enter must observe two conditions\" is a complete clause. \"Two conditions\" sets up the expectation that the colon will introduce what those conditions are, and the list of conditions follows. Both colons appear after complete clauses and introduce content promised by the first clause. Option D is correct — no change is needed. Option A incorrectly suggests replacing the first colon with a semicolon — \"to preserve the written knowledge of the Keep\" is not an independent clause (it begins with \"to,\" making it an infinitive phrase), so a semicolon would be wrong. Option B incorrectly suggests replacing the second colon with a semicolon — the list after the second colon is also not an independent clause standing alone. Option C incorrectly suggests a comma after \"purpose\" — a comma would be far too weak to introduce the specific content of the Crypts' purpose.",
