        "explanation": "\"It's\" (with an apostrophe) is a contraction meaning \"it is\" or \"it has.\" \"Its\" (without an apostrophe) is the possessive pronoun — it shows ownership, like \"his\" or \"her.\" To test which is correct, replace the word with \"it is\" and see if the sentence makes sense. In option B: \"The archive has lost it is record\" — this makes no sense, so the possessive \"its\" is correct. The archive owns the record. Option B is correct. Option A uses \"it's\" but the sentence means the archive owns its record — possessive is needed, so \"it's\" is wrong. Option C uses \"Its\" to start a sentence when \"It's a well-known fact\" is clearly \"It is a well-known fact\" — a contraction (\"It's\") is needed. Option D uses \"it's\" (contraction \"it is\") but the sentence shows the cat owned its paws — possessive \"its\" is needed.",
